 get the two constants from `pngpriv.h` and declare an equivalent `#[repr(C)]` byte array. Exercise every `PNG_NUMBER_FORMAT_*` value, parameter numbers 1..count, oversized strings (truncation), and log the resulting formatted warning message text (it arrives through `cb_warning` into the trace).\n* L13 `png_build_grayscale_palette` — bit depths 1,2,4,8 and also 3 and 16 (the C writes nothing for those); pass a 256*3-byte buffer prefilled with 0xAA and log the whole buffer.\n* L14 `png_convert_to_rfc1123_buffer`, `png_convert_from_time_t`, `png_convert_from_struct_tm` — use the `PngTime` struct from `support::core`. Random times plus out-of-range fields (year 10000, month 0/13, day 0/32, hour 24, minute 60, second 61). For `png_convert_from_struct_tm` you need a `#[repr(C)] struct Tm` matching glibc `struct tm` (9 `c_int` fields, then `c_long tm_gmtoff`, `*const c_char tm_zone`). Log the return value and all output fields / the 29-byte buffer.\n* L15 `png_sig_cmp` — the correct 8-byte signature, every single-byte corruption of it, and all (start, num_to_check) pairs with start 0..9 and num 0..9.\n* L16 `png_calculate_crc`, `png_reset_crc`, `png_crc_error` — these need a read `png_struct`; they use `png_ptr->crc` / `chunk_name` internal state, so drive them as the C does: create a read struct, then for random buffers call `png_reset_crc`, `png_calculate_crc(png_ptr, data, len)` repeatedly, then `png_crc_error`. You cannot read `png_ptr->crc` directly, so make the observable be `png_crc_error()`'s return for a crafted expected-CRC — instead ALSO cover it end-to-end: use `pngbuild::crc32` to compute the true CRC, and additionally exercise `png_set_crc_action` with all 6×6 values on a valid stream (that part just must not diverge). Log every return value.\n* L17 `png_sRGB_table`, `png_sRGB_base`, `png_sRGB_delta` — exported DATA symbols. Use `lib.data(\"png_sRGB_table\")` etc. Sizes: `png_sRGB_table` is 256 `png_uint_16`, `png_sRGB_base` is 512 `png_uint_16`, `png_sRGB_delta` is 512 `png_byte` — VERIFY in `c_src/src/png.c`. Compare the full contents of both libraries.\n* L18 `png_malloc`, `png_calloc`, `png_malloc_warn`, `png_malloc_base`, `png_malloc_array`, `png_realloc_array`, `png_free`, `png_zalloc`, `png_zfree` — sizes 0, 1, 7, 1024, and a huge value (but for the huge/OOM cases only log the null-ness of the result, and remember `png_malloc` errors out on failure → that is Phase C; use `png_malloc_warn`/`png_malloc_base` for the failing sizes). Also drive them with a struct created by `png_create_read_struct_2` + the harness `cb_malloc`/`cb_free` and `with_session(|s| s.trace_alloc = true)` so the allocation sizes are compared. `png_malloc_array`/`png_realloc_array` signatures are in `pngpriv.h`.\n\nRULES (repeat of the important ones):\n* Fixed seeds everywhere; use `Rng::new(<literal>)`.\n* Never log pointer values — only null-ness, sizes and contents.\n* Do NOT edit anything except your new `tests/lowlevel_a.rs`. Never touch `src/`, `c_src/`,\n  `tests/support/*`, `Cargo.toml`.\n* If a test fails because the C and Rust traces genuinely differ, KEEP the test as-is and\n  report the divergence precisely (which function, which input, the two trace lines).\n  Do not `#[ignore]`, do not weaken, do not \"fix\" the Rust.\n* Compile+run with `timeout 600 cargo test --test lowlevel_a 2>&1 | tail -40`. Iterate until it\n  compiles cleanly. Fix your own test bugs (wrong signature, wrong buffer size, logging a\n  pointer, etc.).\n\nFINAL REPORT: list each test fn, whether it passes, and for every FAILING test the exact\ndivergence (C trace line vs Rust trace line, and the input that triggered it).","outputFile":"/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-4DaaTZ/tmp/claude-31072124/-local-home-scheschb--harvest-work-harvest-work-4DaaTZ-translated-rust/11547ad3-7cdc-4bcb-a504-9e6a5631306e/tasks/aa93674a3b9b8d64d.output","canReadOutputFile":true}}
