is initialised lazily by `png_init_filter_functions` from\n  `png_read_filter_row`, so calling it on a fresh read struct is exactly what the C does.\n  Log the filtered row buffer in hex.\n* **L21** `png_do_read_interlace(png_row_infop, png_bytep row, int pass, png_uint_32 transformations)`\n  and `png_do_write_interlace(png_row_infop, png_bytep row, int pass)`.\n  pass 0..6 × bit depth 1,2,4,8,16 × colour types × random rows. For read-interlace the row\n  buffer must be big enough for the expanded row (allocate generously and log the whole\n  buffer). Also pass `transformations` = 0 and = PNG_PACKSWAP (0x00100000 — CHECK the value in\n  `pngpriv.h`) because the C branches on it.\n* **L22** `png_combine_row(png_const_structrp, png_bytep dp, int display)`.\n  This uses png_struct internal state (`row_info`, `pass`, `transformations`, `row_buf`), so\n  drive it the way libpng does: through a real interlaced read using `png_read_row` with BOTH\n  a `row` and a `display_row` buffer (that is the code path that calls `png_combine_row` with\n  display 0 and 1), over every bit depth 1,2,4,8,16 and colour type, width 1..17. Log both\n  buffers after every row. Additionally call `png_progressive_combine_row` in a progressive\n  read row callback for the same images (see `png_set_progressive_read_fn`).\n* **L23** `png_access_version_number`, `png_get_copyright`, `png_get_header_ver`,\n  `png_get_libpng_ver`, `png_get_header_version` — with NULL and with a real png_ptr.\n* **L24** `png_permit_mng_features(png_structrp, png_uint_32 mng_features_permitted)` — every\n  bit of the mask 0..0x07 plus 0xffffffff, on read and write structs; log the return value.\n  `png_set_option(png_structrp, int option, int onoff)` — option 0..12 (and 13, -1, 99 as\n  out-of-range) × onoff PNG_OPTION_OFF/ON/UNSET (0,1,2) and an invalid 3; log the return.\n* **L25** `png_icc_check_header`, `png_icc_check_length`, `png_icc_check_tag_table` — signatures\n  in `pngpriv.h`. Build synthetic ICC profiles in Rust: a minimal *valid* one (132-byte header +\n  tag table; look at `png_icc_check_*` in `c_src/src/png.c` to see exactly what is validated —\n  size, tag count, signature 'acsp', profile/device class, colour space, PCS, rendering\n  intent, tag table entries) and then a family of mutations (each header field wrong, bad tag\n  offsets/lengths, overlapping tags). Log the return value and all messages. Drive them with a\n  read struct and `png_set_benign_errors(1)` so an error does not abort the whole trace early\n  (do BOTH: benign on and off, in separate `diff` runs).\n* **L26** `png_build_gamma_table(png_structrp, int bit_depth)` and `png_destroy_gamma_table`.\n  These read `png_ptr->gamma`/`screen_gamma`, which are set by `png_set_gamma`. Drive them via\n  a real read: build 8-bit and 16-bit PNGs with a gAMA chunk, call `png_set_gamma` with\n  (screen, file) pairs {1.0, 0.45455, 2.2, 100000-fixed extremes}, read all rows and compare\n  (the gamma tables are only observable through the transformed rows — that is the point).\n  Then also call `png_build_gamma_table` DIRECTLY after `png_read_info` + `png_set_gamma` for\n  bit_depth 8 and 16 and log the resulting rows.\n* **L27** `png_info_init_3`, `png_create_info_struct`, `png_destroy_info_struct`,\n  `png_data_freer`, `png_free_data`. Populate an info struct with several chunks\n  (`png_set_PLTE`, `png_set_tRNS`, `png_set_text`, `png_set_unknown_chunks`, `png_set_rows`),\n  then exercise `png_free_data` with each individual `PNG_FREE_*` mask and `PNG_FREE_ALL`,\n  `num` -1 and 0, and `png_data_freer` with DESTROY/SET/USER × masks; after each step log\n  `png_get_valid` for every flag and the getters for the affected chunks. Also\n  `png_info_init_3(&info, sizeof png_info)` — get the right size by comparing what the C\n  accepts: it warns/errors if the size differs, so use `png_create_info_struct` to obtain a\n  block and pass the correct `size_of` value (find `png_info` size handling in `pngset.c`/\n  `png.c`; the C compares against its own `sizeof(png_info)`), and also pass a deliberately\n  wrong size to see the identical message.\n* **L28** `png_check_IHDR(png_const_structrp, png_uint_32 width, png_uint_32 height, int bit_depth, int color_type, int interlace_type, int compression_type, int filter_type)`.\n  Call it DIRECTLY on a read struct for the full cross product: width {0,1,7,8,0x7fffffff,\n  0x80000000}, height {0,1,8}, bit_depth {0,1,2,3,4,7,8,16,32}, colour type {0,1,2,3,4,5,6,7},\n  interlace {0,1,2}, compression {0,1}, filter {0,1,64}. That is a lot — prune to a\n  deterministic random sample of ~400 combinations plus all 15 legal (colour,depth) pairs.\n  Every message (they are warnings/errors) lands in the trace; use `png_set_benign_errors(1)`\n  in one run and 0 in another. png_error longjmps, so structure the test as ONE\n  `diff`/`protected` run PER combination so an early exit does not hide later combinations.\n* **L29** `png_zstream_error`, `png_reset_zstream`, `png_inflate_claim`, `png_zlib_inflate`,\n  `png_inflate` — read the C in `c_src/src/pngrutil.c`. Drive them through real inflate work:\n  build PNGs whose IDAT/zTXt/iCCP zlib streams are (a) valid stored-deflate,\n  (b) valid multi-block, (c) truncated, (d) with a bad adler32, (e) with an invalid CMF byte,\n  using `pngbuild::zlib_stored` and hand-built variants, then read them. Also call\n  `png_reset_zstream` directly and log its return code. Every zlib message must match.\n\nRULES:\n* Fixed seeds only (`Rng::new(<literal>)`).\n* Never log a pointer/address — only null-ness, sizes, contents.\n* Do NOT edit anything except your new `tests/lowlevel_b.rs`. Never touch `src/`, `c_src/`,\n  `tests/support/*`, `Cargo.toml`, or other test files.\n* If C and Rust genuinely diverge: KEEP the test, do NOT weaken it, do NOT `#[ignore]` it,\n  do NOT modify `src/`. Report it.\n* Run `timeout 600 cargo test --test lowlevel_b 2>&1 | tail -40`; iterate until it compiles and\n  only genuine divergences remain