 and when the double is outside\nthe destination's range the result is the \"integer indefinite\" value `INT_MIN` (0x80000000)\ntruncated. Rust's `as` cast from `f64` to an integer **saturates** instead. So the two differ\nfor every out-of-range double.\n\nConfirmed live divergence (differential test `tests/lowlevel_a.rs::l6_gamma_helpers`):\n\n```\npng_gamma_16bit_correct(value=1, gamma_val=-1)\n  C   -> 6        (double r == 65542.0 ; (png_uint_16)r == 65542 & 0xffff == 6)\n  RUST-> 65535    (65542.0 as u16 saturates)\n```\nMore confirmed cases:\n| call | C | Rust (wrong) |\n|---|---|---|\n| `png_gamma_16bit_correct(127, -1)` | 3 | 65535 |\n| `png_gamma_16bit_correct(65534, INT32_MIN)` | 25411 | 65535 |\n| `png_gamma_16bit_correct(1, INT32_MIN)` | 0 | 65535 |\n| `png_gamma_8bit_correct(1, INT32_MIN)` | 0 | 255 |\n| `png_gamma_8bit_correct(254, INT32_MIN)` | 0 | 255 |\n\n## Task\n\n### 1. Add exact-emulation helpers\n\nIn `src/helpers.rs` (it is re-exported through `crate::prelude`, check how the existing\nhelpers there are declared and follow the same style) add:\n\n```rust\n/// x86-64 `cvttsd2si` (32-bit): truncate toward zero; NaN or out-of-range\n/// produces the \"integer indefinite\" value `i32::MIN`.  This is what gcc emits\n/// for a C cast from `double` to any signed/unsigned integer type of 32 bits or\n/// less, so the C behaviour for out-of-range doubles is reproduced exactly\n/// (Rust's own `as` cast saturates instead).\n#[inline]\npub fn c_f64_to_i32(v: f64) -> i32 { ... }\n\n/// x86-64 `cvttsd2siq` (64-bit) — used for C casts to 64-bit integer types.\n#[inline]\npub fn c_f64_to_i64(v: f64) -> i64 { ... }\n```\nImplement them as: NaN or `v < -2147483648.0` or `v >= 2147483648.0` → `i32::MIN`, else\n`v as i32` (and analogously for i64 with -9223372036854775808.0 / 9223372036854775808.0).\nMake sure the boundary comparisons are correct (`>=` on the positive side, `<` on the negative\nside — `-2147483648.0` itself IS representable).\n\nThen the C cast `(T)double_expr` for T of width ≤ 32 bits becomes\n`c_f64_to_i32(double_expr) as T`, and for 64-bit T `c_f64_to_i64(double_expr) as T`.\n\n### 2. Fix EVERY site\n\nFind every place in `src/*.rs` where a value that is an `f64`/`c_double` is converted to an\ninteger type with `as`. Search systematically, e.g.\n\n```bash\ngrep -rn \" as png_byte\\| as png_uint_16\\| as png_uint_32\\| as png_int_32\\| as png_fixed_point\\| as c_int\\| as c_uint\\| as u8\\| as u16\\| as u32\\| as i32\\| as i64\\| as usize\\| as size_t\" src/*.rs\n```\nand for each hit determine the *static type of the operand* (only fix it when the operand is a\nfloating-point value — `f64`, `c_double`, the result of `floor/pow/modf/frexp/sqrt/fabs`, a\n`* 1.0` expression, etc.). Cross-check the corresponding C line in `c_src/src/*.c` (the Rust\nfiles are 1:1 translations: `src/png.rs` ↔ `c_src/src/png.c`, `src/pngrtran.rs` ↔\n`c_src/src/pngrtran.c`, `src/pngrutil_a1.rs`/`_a2`/`_b1a`/`_b1b`/`_b2` ↔ `c_src/src/pngrutil.c`,\netc.) to confirm the C really performs a double→integer cast there, and what the destination\ntype is.\n\nKnown sites to start from (verify each, there may be more):\n* `src/png.rs:2215` `return r as png_byte;` (png_gamma_8bit_correct)\n* `src/png.rs:2230` `return r as png_uint_16;` (png_gamma_16bit_correct)\n* `src/png.rs:2127`, `2142`, `2165`, `2179`, `2193` (`png_fixed`, `png_fixed_ITU`,\n  `png_muldiv`-adjacent, `png_reciprocal`, `png_reciprocal2` — check the C in\n  `c_src/src/png.c`; note some of these are guarded by explicit range checks in the C, in which\n  case the behaviour is already identical and you should leave them alone but say so)\n* `src/png.rs:2060` (`png_ascii_from_fp`/`png_format_number` area), `src/png.rs:1963`,\n  `src/png.rs:2277`, `src/png.rs:1539`\n* `src/pngget.rs:292`\n* `src/pngrtran.rs` gamma/quantize/background sites (e.g. lines ~724, ~1334, ~2991, ~3211, ~3278)\n* `src/pngset.rs:225`\n* Any site in `src/pngwutil.rs`, `src/pngwtran.rs`, `src/pngrutil_*.rs`\n\n**Do not change casts whose operand is an integer** — those are C integer conversions and Rust's\n`as` already matches (wrapping truncation).\n**Do not change behaviour for in-range values** — `c_f64_to_i32(x) as T` is identical to\n`x as T` whenever x is in range, so this is a pure no-op for all currently passing tests.\n\n### 3. Verify\n\n```bash\ncd /local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-4DaaTZ/translated_rust\ntimeout 600 cargo build 2>&1 | tail -5\ntimeout 600 cargo test --test lowlevel_a 2>&1 | tail -25   # l6_gamma_helpers must now pass\ntimeout 600 cargo test --test smoke --test write_a 2>&1 | tail -10   # must stay green\n```\n`l6_gamma_helpers` MUST pass and no previously passing test may break.\nDo NOT edit any file under `tests/` and do NOT edit `c_src/`. Do not \"fix\" the test.\n\nFINAL REPORT: the helper code you added, a table of every site you changed\n(file:line, the C line it corresponds to, destination type) and every site you deliberately did\nNOT change with the reason, plus the test results.","agentId":"af3329a414a09f5cb","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"All done. Final verification summary:\n\n## Helper code added (`src/helpers.rs`, lines 86-110)\n\n```rust\n/// x86-64 `cvttsd2si` (32-bit): truncate toward zero; NaN or out-of-range\n/// produces the \"integer indefinite\" value `i32::MIN`.  This is what gcc emits\n/// for a C cast from `double` to any signed/unsigned integer type of 32 bits or\n/// less, so the C behaviour for out-of-range doubles is reproduced exactly\n/// (Rust's own `as` cast saturates instead).\n#[inline]\npub fn c_f64_to_i32(v: f64) -> i32 {\n    if v.is_nan() || v < -2147483648.0 || v >= 2147483648.0 { i32::MIN } else { v as i32 }\n}\n\n/// x86-64 `cvttsd2siq` (64-bit) — used for C casts to 64-bit integer types.\n#[inline]\npub fn c_f64_to_i64(v: f64) -> i64 {\n    if v.is_nan() || v < -9223372036854775808.0 || v >= 9223372036854775808.0 { i64::MIN } else { v as i64 }\n}\n```\n`helpers` is already re-exported via `crate::prelude`, so no import changes were needed. `c_f64_to_i64` has no call sites (no C cast in libpng targets a 64-bit integer from a double); it is covered by the existing `#![allow(dead_code)]` at the top of the file.\n\n## How I enumerated the sites (exhaustive, both directions)\n\nRust side: only 7 files mention `f64`/`c_double` at all, and only 4 contain float arithmetic (`src/png.rs`, `src/pngget.rs`, `src/pngset.rs`, `src/pngrtran.rs`). A grep for decimal/exponent float literals plus `f32`/`f64` over the remaining 20 files returned only comments and version strings — so `pngwutil.rs`, `pngwtran.rs`, `pngrutil_*.rs`, `pngread.rs`, `pngwrite.rs` etc. contain **no** floating-point values and therefore no float→int casts.\n\nC side cross-check: `grep -nE \"\\((png_byte|png_uint_16|png_uint_32|png_int_32|png_fixed_point|int|...)\\) *\\(?(r|d|dd|fp|base|mult|test|fmax|output_gamma|res_x|res_y)\\b\" c_src/src/*.c` produced the complete candidate list, which I then typed one by one. The result matches the Rust-side enumeration exactly.\n\n## Sites changed\n\n| Rust file:line | C line | expression | dest type |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `src/png.rs:1963` | `png.c:2537` `*ascii++ = (char)(48 + (int)d);` | `(48 + c_f64_to_i32(d)) as c_char` | `int` (then `char`) |\n| `src/png.rs:2127` | `png.c:2737` `return (png_fixed_point)r;` (`png_fixed`) | `c_f64_to_i32(r) as png_fixed_point` | `png_fixed_point` |\n| `src/png.rs:2142` | `png.c:2756` `return (png_uint_32)r;` (`png_fixed_ITU`) | `c_f64_to_i32(r) as png_uint_32` | `png_uint_32` |\n| `src/png.rs:2215` | `png.c:3303` `return (png_byte)r;` (`png_gamma_8bit_correct`) | `c_f64_to_i32(r) as png_byte` | `png_byte` |\n| `src/png.rs:2230` | `png.c:3333` `return (png_uint_16)r;` (`png_gamma_16bit_correct`) | `c_f64_to_i32(r) as png_uint_16` | `png_uint_16` |\n| `src/png.rs:2277` | `png.c:3427` `sub_table[j] = (png_uint_16)d;` (`png_build_16bit_table`) | `c_f64_to_i32(d) as png_uint_16` | `png_uint_16` |\n| `src/pngget.rs:407` | `pngget.c:464` `*res_x = (png_uint_32)(*res_x * .0254 + .50);` | `c_f64_to_i32(...) as png_uint_32` | `png_uint_32` |\n| `src/pngget.rs:410` | `pngget.c:465` `*res_y = (png_uint_32)(*res_y * .0254 + .50);` | `c_f64_to_i32(...) as png_uint_32` | `png_uint_32` |\n| `src/pngrtran.rs:251` | `pngrtran.c:327` `return (png_fixed_point)output_gamma;` (`convert_gamma_value`) | `c_f64_to_i32(output_gamma) as png_fixed_point` | `png_fixed_point` |\n\n## Sites deliberately NOT changed\n\n**Guarded so the cast is unreachable when out of range** — these I left alone, as suggested:\n| Rust | C | reason |\n|---|---|---|\n| `src/png.rs:2165` (`png_muldiv`) | `png.c:2792` | guard is `if (r <= 2147483647. && r >= -2147483648.)`; the cast only runs *inside* the true branch, and NaN makes the `&&` false, so C and Rust are already bit-identical for every input including NaN/Inf. |\n| `src/png.rs:2179` (`png_reciprocal`) | `png.c:2892` | same positive-guard form. |\n| `src/png.rs:2193` (`png_reciprocal2`) | `png.c:2963` | same positive-guard form. |\n\n**Note on the two guarded sites I *did* change** (`png.rs:2127`, `png.rs:2142`): unlike the three above, `png_fixed`/`png_fixed_ITU` use the *inverse* guard (`if (r > 2147483647. || r < -2147483648.) png_fixed_error(...)`). NaN is false for both comparisons, so a NaN argument slips past the check and reaches the cast — C yields `INT_MIN`, Rust `as` yielded `0`. So these were genuinely divergent (for NaN) and needed the helper. For every non-NaN input the change is a strict no-op.\n\n**Operand is an integer, not a float** (C integer conversion; Rust `as` already matches):\n* `src/png.rs:2060` — `num = fp as png_uint_32`, `fp: png_fixed_point` (C `png.c:2659`, `fp` is `png_fixed_point`).\n* `src/png.rs:1539/1540` — `r`/`g` are `png_fixed_point` (C `png.c:1943`).\n* `src/pngget.rs:292` — `result as png_uint_32`, `result: png_fixed_point`.\n* `src/pngset.rs:225` (`png_ITU_fixed_16`) — `v as png_uint_16`, `v: png_fixed_point`.\n* `src/pngrtran.rs:724, 1334, 2991, 3211, 3278` — all operands are `c_int`/`png_byte`/`png_uint_16`/`png_uint_32`. The one that looked most suspicious, `pngrtran.c:878` `distance[d_index] = (png_byte)d;`, has `int d = dmax + dt + db;` in C. Likewise `pngwutil.c:2137/2146/2175/2183/2211/2219` `(png_byte)d` all have `int d` (`png_do_write_interlace`).\n\n**Float→float, not float→int:** `src/pngget.rs:366, 377` (`... as f32`, C `(float)`).\n\n**No `png_product2` in the Rust translation** — `png.c:2939` sits inside `#ifndef PNG_FLOATING_ARITHMETIC_SUPPORTED` nested in `#ifdef PNG_FLOATING_ARITHMETIC_SUPPORTED`, i.e. dead code, correctly not translated.\n\n## Test results\n\n```\nlowlevel_a: ok.  18 passed; 0 failed   <-- l6_gamma_helpers now PASSES\nlowlevel_b: ok.  30 passed; 0 failed\nsmoke:      ok.   4 passed; 0 failed\nwrite_a:    ok.  12 passed; 0 failed\nread_a:     FAILED. 14 passed; 2 failed  (r4_read_png_transforms, r16_byte_order_transforms)\n```\n\nTwo caveats you should know about, neither caused by this change:\n\n1. **`read_a`'s 2 failures are pre-existing.** I proved this with an A/B experiment: I temporarily reduced `c_f64_to_i32` to plain `v as i32` (restoring the old saturating behaviour), rebuilt, and re-ran `read_a` — the *same* two tests (`r4_read_png_transforms`, `r16_byte_order_transforms`) failed identically. I then restored the real helper.\n2. **`tests/chunks.rs` does not compile**, so a bare `cargo test` aborts before running anything. This file (and `tests/lowlevel_b.rs`) appeared in `tests/` after I started, and `chunks.rs`'s errors are unrelated to this work: `E0768 no valid digits found for number`, `E0425 cannot find value PNG_SCAL_METER in this scope` (a missing const in `src/consts.rs`), and two `E0308` arg-type mismatches passing `i32` `SRGB_XY[..]` where `u32` is expected. Per instructions I did not touch anything under `tests/`. If someone else is mid-way through adding `PNG_SCAL_METER` and the `png_set_mDCV_fixed` signature, that's the fix — it is a separate defect from the double→int cast bug."}],"resolvedModel":"global.anthropic.claude-opus-5[1m]","totalDurationMs":343162,"totalTokens":88889,"totalToolUseCount":51,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1698,"cache_read_input_tokens":83687,"output_tokens":3502,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":179},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1698},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":4,"searchCount":0,"bashCount":37,"editFileCount":10,"linesAdded":55,"linesRemoved":30,"otherToolCount":0}}}
