of hand-rolled struct creation inside `protected`). The callbacks cannot easily see the current\nlibrary, so pass the needed function pointers through a `thread_local` raw pointer to a small\n`#[repr(C)]` context struct that you set before each run (`png_get_progressive_ptr` also returns\nthe user pointer you passed — use it for P8).\n\nIn the row callback you MUST log: `row_num`, `pass`, whether `new_row` is NULL, and the row bytes\n(`hex`) — and for interlaced images call `png_progressive_combine_row(png, dst_row, new_row)`\ninto a persistent destination image buffer and log the destination row afterwards. In the info\ncallback log everything with `log_all_info(c, png, info)` plus `png_get_rowbytes`. In the end\ncallback log `log_all_info` again.\n\nRows:\n* **P1** feed sizes 1, 2, 3, 7, 13, 64 and the whole file at once × all 15 legal\n  (colour_type, bit_depth) combos, interlace 0, widths 1, 7, 17, heights 1, 5.\n  (15 legal combos: GRAY 1/2/4/8/16, RGB 8/16, PALETTE 1/2/4/8, GRAY_ALPHA 8/16, RGB_ALPHA 8/16.)\n* **P2** interlaced (Adam7) input, all combos, feed sizes 1, 5, 64, whole file;\n  `png_progressive_combine_row` into a full-image buffer; log every intermediate destination row\n  so the incremental composition is compared.\n* **P3** `png_process_data_pause(png, save)` with save = 0 and save = 1: call it from inside the\n  info callback and from inside the row callback (that is the documented use), then resume by\n  continuing to feed data (respecting the returned number of unprocessed bytes — read the C in\n  `c_src/src/pngpread.c` and the comment in `png.h` carefully; log the return value each time).\n* **P4** `png_process_data_skip`: after a pause inside a callback, log the return value and skip\n  that many input bytes; use an input with a large unknown chunk and with a large tEXt chunk.\n* **P5** transforms set in the info callback: `png_set_expand`, `png_set_gray_to_rgb`,\n  `png_set_strip_16`, `png_set_packing`, `png_set_gamma(2.2, 0.45455)`, `png_set_bgr`, and\n  `png_read_update_info` — several combinations × several colour types; log the rows.\n* **P6** IDAT split into many chunks: rebuild a valid PNG with `pngbuild::split`/`join` so the\n  zlib stream is spread over IDAT chunks of 1, 2 and 3 bytes, plus a zero-length IDAT chunk\n  inserted in the middle and at the start/end of the IDAT run (all legal PNG).\n* **P7** an input carrying ancillary chunks before and after IDAT (gAMA, cHRM, sBIT, bKGD, tEXt,\n  zTXt, tIME, unknown private chunks) × `png_set_keep_unknown_chunks` with\n  PNG_HANDLE_CHUNK_AS_DEFAULT/NEVER/IF_SAFE/ALWAYS × `png_set_read_user_chunk_fn` with a\n  callback returning -1, 0 and 1 (log the chunk name/size/data it receives).\n* **P8** `png_get_progressive_ptr`: log that it returns exactly the pointer you passed\n  (compare `ptr == expected`, log the boolean only — never the address itself).\n\nRULES:\n* Fixed seeds only (`Rng::new(<literal>)`); several inputs per configuration.\n* Never log a pointer/address — only null-ness / equality booleans, sizes, contents.\n* Do NOT edit anything except your new `tests/progressive.rs`. Never touch `src/`, `c_src/`,\n  `tests/support/*`, `Cargo.toml`, or other test files (other agents are editing those).\n* If C and Rust genuinely diverge: KEEP the test, do NOT weaken it, do NOT `#[ignore]`, do NOT\n  modify `src/`. Report it precisely.\n* **`cargo test` does NOT rebuild the cdylib.** Always run\n  `timeout 600 cargo build && timeout 600 cargo test --test progressive 2>&1 | tail -40`.\n* Iterate until it compiles and only genuine divergences remain. Runtime under ~90 s — feed size 1\n  over a big image is slow, so keep those images tiny (≤ 8×4).\n\nFINAL REPORT: per test fn — pass/fail, and for each failure the exact C-vs-Rust trace difference\nplus the triggering configuration."}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2010,"cache_read_input_tokens":265489,"cache_creation"