{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_0135pzU4Kq7R21zgW5CUxp84","type":"tool_result","content":"typedef PNG_CALLBACK(void, *png_rw_ptr,\n   (png_structp, png_bytep, size_t));\ntypedef PNG_CALLBACK(void, *png_flush_ptr,\n   (png_structp));\ntypedef PNG_CALLBACK(void, *png_read_status_ptr,\n   (png_structp, png_uint_32, int));\ntypedef PNG_CALLBACK(void, *png_write_status_ptr,\n   (png_structp, png_uint_32, int));\n\n#ifdef PNG_PROGRESSIVE_READ_SUPPORTED\ntypedef PNG_CALLBACK(void, *png_progressive_info_ptr,\n   (png_structp, png_infop));\ntypedef PNG_CALLBACK(void, *png_progressive_end_ptr,\n   (png_structp, png_infop));\n\n/* The following callback receives png_uint_32 row_number, int pass for the\n * png_bytep data of the row.  When transforming an interlaced image the\n * row number is the row number within the sub-image of the interlace pass, so\n * the value will increase to the height of the sub-image (not the full image)\n * then reset to 0 for the next pass.\n *\n * Use PNG_ROW_FROM_PASS_ROW(row, pass) and PNG_COL_FROM_PASS_COL(col, pass) to\n * find the output pixel (x,y) given an interlaced sub-image pixel\n * (row,col,pass).  (See below for these macros.)\n */\ntypedef PNG_CALLBACK(void, *png_progressive_row_ptr,\n   (png_structp, png_bytep, png_uint_32, int));\n#endif\n\n#if defined(PNG_READ_USER_TRANSFORM_SUPPORTED) || \\\n    defined(PNG_WRITE_USER_TRANSFORM_SUPPORTED)\ntypedef PNG_CALLBACK(void, *png_user_transform_ptr,\n   (png_structp, png_row_infop, png_bytep));\n#endif\n\n#ifdef PNG_USER_CHUNKS_SUPPORTED\ntypedef PNG_CALLBACK(int, *png_user_chunk_ptr,\n   (png_structp, png_unknown_chunkp));\n#endif\n#ifdef PNG_UNKNOWN_CHUNKS_SUPPORTED\n/* not used anywhere */\n---\n\n#ifdef PNG_PROGRESSIVE_READ_SUPPORTED\n/* Sets the function callbacks for the push reader, and a pointer to a\n * user-defined structure available to the callback functions.\n */\nPNG_EXPORT(90, void, png_set_progressive_read_fn,\n   (png_structrp png_ptr,\n    png_voidp progressive_ptr, png_progressive_info_ptr info_fn,\n    png_progressive_row_ptr row_fn, png_progressive_end_ptr end_fn));\n\n/* Returns the user pointer associated with the push read functions */\nPNG_EXPORT(91, png_voidp, png_get_progressive_ptr,\n   (png_const_structrp png_ptr));\n\n/* Function to be called when data becomes available */\nPNG_EXPORT(92, void, png_process_data,\n   (png_structrp png_ptr,\n    png_inforp info_ptr, png_bytep buffer, size_t buffer_size));\n\n/* A function which may be called *only* within png_process_data to stop the\n * processing of any more data.  The function returns the number of bytes\n * remaining, excluding any that libpng has cached internally.  A subsequent\n * call to png_process_data must supply these bytes again.  If the argument\n * 'save' is set to true the routine will first save all the pending data and\n * will always return 0.\n */\nPNG_EXPORT(219, size_t, png_process_data_pause,\n   (png_structrp, int save));\n\n/* A function which may be called *only* outside (after) a call to\n * png_process_data.  It returns the number of bytes of data to skip in the\n * input.  Normally it will return 0, but if it returns a non-zero value the\n * application must skip than number of bytes of input data and pass the\n * following data to the next call to png_process_data.\n */\nPNG_EXPORT(220, png_uint_32, png_process_data_skip,\n   (png_structrp));\n\n/* Function that combines rows.  'new_row' is a flag that should come from\n * the callback and be non-NULL if anything needs to be done; the library\n * stores its own version of the new data internally and ignores the passed\n * in value.\n */\nPNG_EXPORT(93, void, png_progressive_combine_row,\n   (png_const_structrp png_ptr,\n    png_bytep old_row, png_const_bytep new_row));\n#endif /* PROGRESSIVE_READ */\n\nPNG_EXPORTA(94, png_voidp, png_malloc,\n   (png_const_structrp png_ptr, png_alloc_size_t size),\n   PNG_ALLOCATED);\n/* Added at libpng version 1.4.0 */\nPNG_EXPORTA(95, png_voidp, png_calloc,\n   (png_const_structrp png_ptr, png_alloc_size_t size),\n   PNG_ALLOCATED);","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_01AxwS3LQDAQG2Gmsh4CrGkB","session_id":"11547ad3-7cdc-4bcb-a504-9e6a5631306e","uuid":"cb5b75f6-a767-4b17-aa1c-ebb498646482","timestamp":"2026-08-14T15:44:51.859Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Write progressive tests P1-P8"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_zx3whnpv63fhv5npfo6pou3tkbur5eqxbqfdnm6tmdhnnzij5uzq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_bdrk_01Q6KZeTob5KWmg2bn2G4oMT","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"Write simplified API tests S1-S7","prompt":"You are writing ONE Rust integration test file for a C→Rust differential verification of libpng 1.6.59.\n\nWorking dir: `/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-4DaaTZ/translated_rust`\n\nFIRST read (short): `tests/HARNESS.md`, `tests/support/core.rs` (note `PngImage`, all\n`PNG_FORMAT_*` and `PNG_IMAGE_*` constants are already defined there), `tests/read_a.rs` (a\npassing example), and the \"S\" section of `CONFIGS.md`. You implement rows **S1 through S7** —\nthe simplified API (`png_image_*`, implemented in the simplified sections of\n`c_src/src/pngread.c` and `c_src/src/pngwrite.c`).\n\nCREATE exactly one new file: `tests/simplified.rs`, one `#[test]` fn per row (`s1_...` … `s7_...`).\nEvery test drives BOTH `.so`s with `diff(...)` on IDENTICAL inputs and compares the full trace\nbyte-for-byte.\n\n## Pattern\n\nThe simplified API needs NO png_struct, NO error callback and NO longjmp pad from us — it has\nits own internal `png_safe_execute`/`setjmp`. So the tests are simple direct calls:\n\n```rust\nlet mut image = PngImage::default();          // opaque=NULL, version=1\nimage.version = PNG_IMAGE_VERSION;\nlet rc = (begin_read_from_memory)(&mut image as *mut PngImage as *mut c_void,\n                                  png.as_ptr() as *const c_void, png.len());\nlog(format!(\"begin rc={rc} w={} h={} fmt={:#x} flags={:#x} cmap={} woe={} msg={}\",\n    image.width, image.height, image.format, image.flags,\n    image.colormap_entries, image.warning_or_error, image.msg()));\nimage.format = PNG_FORMAT_RGBA;               // request an output format\nlet stride = /* PNG_IMAGE_ROW_STRIDE equivalent */;\nlet mut buf = vec![0u8; /* PNG_IMAGE_BUFFER_SIZE equivalent */];\nlet rc = (finish_read)(&mut image as *mut _ as *mut c_void, background_ptr,\n                       buf.as_mut_ptr() as *mut c_void, stride, colormap_ptr);\nlog(format!(\"finish rc={rc} woe={} msg={}\", image.warning_or_error, image.msg()));\nlog(format!(\"buf={}\", hex(&buf)));\n(image_free)(&mut image as *mut _ as *mut c_void);\n```\nResolve the entry points with `lib.f(...)` using the EXACT signatures from\n`c_src/include/png.h`: `png_image_begin_read_from_memory`, `png_image_begin_read_from_file`,\n`png_image_begin_read_from_stdio`, `png_image_finish_read`, `png_image_free`,\n`png_image_write_to_memory`, `png_image_write_to_file`, `png_image_write_to_stdio`.\n\nYou must compute the buffer size / row stride exactly like the `PNG_IMAGE_*` macros in `png.h`\ndo (read them: `PNG_IMAGE_PIXEL_CHANNELS`, `PNG_IMAGE_PIXEL_COMPONENT_SIZE`,\n`PNG_IMAGE_ROW_STRIDE`, `PNG_IMAGE_BUFFER_SIZE`, `PNG_IMAGE_COLORMAP_SIZE`,\n`PNG_IMAGE_SIZE`) — implement them as Rust fns in your test file and always allocate 64 bytes of\nslack after the required size, logging the slack separately so an overrun shows up.\n\nBuild the PNG inputs with `support::pngbuild` (`Builder::new(w,h,depth,color)...build_valid(seed)`)\nso both libraries get identical bytes.\n\nRows:\n* **S1** `png_image_begin_read_from_memory` + `png_image_finish_read`: input = every legal\n  (colour_type, bit_depth) combo × interlace 0/1 (GRAY 1/2/4/8/16, RGB 8/16, PALETTE 1/2/4/8,\n  GRAY_ALPHA 8/16, RGB_ALPHA 8/16; palette inputs need a PLTE chunk), output format ∈\n  {GRAY, GA, AG, RGB, BGR, RGBA, ARGB, BGRA, ABGR}. Also cover inputs carrying tRNS and gAMA.\n* **S2** the same but with the LINEAR output formats {LINEAR_Y, LINEAR_Y_ALPHA, LINEAR_RGB,\n  LINEAR_RGB_ALPHA} (2-byte components), several input combos.\n* **S3** the COLORMAP output formats {RGB_COLORMAP, BGR_COLORMAP, RGBA_COLORMAP, ARGB_COLORMAP,\n  BGRA_COLORMAP, ABGR_COLORMAP} — you must also pass a colormap buffer of\n  `PNG_IMAGE_COLORMAP_SIZE` bytes; log `image.colormap_entries` and the whole colormap.\n* **S4** `png_image_finish_read` with a non-NULL `background` (`png_color` = 3 bytes: red,\n  green, blue — define a `#[repr(C)]` struct) on alpha-bearing and tRNS-bearing inputs, × the\n  colour formats without an alpha channel (that is when the background is used), plus a\n  NEGATIVE `row_stride` (bottom-up image) for several formats.\n* **S5** `png_image_write_to_memory`: for every format × `convert_to_8_bit` 0 and 1, first call\n  with `memory = NULL` to query the size (log the returned `memory_bytes`), then allocate and\n  call again; also call with a deliberately too-small `memory_bytes` (the C returns 0 with a\n  specific message — keep it, the message must match) and with a colormapped image + colormap.\n  Build the input pixel buffer yourself with a fixed seed.\n* **S6** the stdio/file variants: `png_image_write_to_file`, `png_image_write_to_stdio`,\n  `png_image_begin_read_from_file`, `png_image_begin_read_from_stdio` using real temporary files\n  under `std::env::temp_dir()` with DISTINCT names per library run (e.g. include the\n  `lib.tag`, which is `\"C\"` or `\"RUST\"`, in the filename so the two runs cannot interfere) —\n  then log the file CONTENTS (hex) so the two libraries' output files are compared, and read\n  them back. For the stdio variants open the file with libc `fopen` (declare it yourself) and\n  pass the `FILE*`.\n* **S7** `png_image_free`: after a successful read, after a failed read (e.g. truncated input),\n  and twice in a row; also `png_image_free` on a zeroed `PngImage` and on one where only\n  `version` is set. Log `image.opaque` NULL-ness (never the address) and `warning_or_error`\n  before/after.\n\nRULES:\n* Fixed seeds only (`Rng::new(<literal>)`); several inputs per configuration.\n* Never log a pointer/address — only null-ness, sizes, contents.\n* `PngImage` must be at a stable address while libpng holds it (`image.opaque` points to\n  internal state) — keep it in a local `let mut image` and pass `&mut image as *mut _`.\n* Do NOT edit anything except your new `tests/simplified.rs`. Never touch `src/`, `c_src/`,\n  `tests/support/*`, `Cargo.toml`, or other test files (other agents are editing those).\n* If C and Rust genuinely diverge: KEEP the test, do NOT weaken it, do NOT `#[ignore]`, do NOT\n  modify `src/`. Report it precisely.\n* **`cargo test` does NOT rebuild the cdylib.** Always run\n  `timeout 600 cargo build && timeout 600 cargo test --test simplified 2>&1 | tail -40`.\n* Iterate until it compiles and only genuine divergences remain. Runtime under ~90 s\n  (images ≤ 16×8).\n\nFINAL REPORT: per test fn — pass/fail, and for each failure the exact C-vs-Rust trace difference\nplus the triggering configuration."}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2924,"cache_read_input_tokens":267499,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2924,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":17,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"11547ad3-7cdc-4bcb-a504-9e6a5631306e","uuid":"4725c2b9-6544-4093-af68-dfe5302e3ac0","timestamp":"2026-08-14T15:44:52.024Z"}
